A maintainer on the project miruamel/zhi (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #220: bug(orch): two conflicting topoSort implementations — one throws on cycle, one records and continues

## Bug

`engine/orch/runner/dag.ts` — two conflicting topological sort implementations with different cycle behavior:

**`topologicalSort`** (line 10) — records cycle, continues:
```ts
if (temp.has(id)) {
  const cycleStart = path.indexOf(id);
  cycles.push(path.slice(cycleStart));
  return;  // ← continues visiting other nodes
}
```

**`topoSort`** (line 80) — throws immediately:
```ts
if (temp.has(id)) {
  cycle.push(...path.slice(start));
  return false;  // ← propagates up
}
// ...
for (const id of nodeIds) {
  if (!visit(id, [])) {
    throw new CycleError(cycle);  // ← throws on first cycle
  }
}
```

Both exist in the same file. `DefaultOrchestratorRunner.run()` uses `topologicalSort` (runner.ts:36) — which silently continues past cycles. A cyclic graph produces an `order` with a cycle embedded in it, and `hasCycles: true` is set, but execution continues.

If `topoSort` were used instead, the runner would throw `CycleError` immediately.

## Exploitation

```ts
const dag = buildDag({ raw: "a,b;a", tokens: [], constraints: [] });
// cyclic edges: s0→s1, s1→s0
const runner = new DefaultOrchestratorRunner();
const result = await runner.run(dag, {});
// topologicalSort continues past cycle → executes with wrong order
// hasCycles = true but no error thrown
```

## Fix

Remove `topoSort` (dead code), or merge both into one implementation with a configurable policy.


Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""A native Jac model of conflicting lenient and strict cycle policies in a dependency graph."""

node Workflow {
    has title: str = "";
    has year: int = 0;
    has available: bool = True;
}

node Task {
    has title: str = "";
    has year: int = 0;
    has available: bool = True;
}

edge DependsOn {
    has reason: str = "";
}

walker RecordingTopo {
    has first_task: str = "";
    has order: list[str] = [];
    has cycles: int = 0;

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can enter with Workflow entry {
        visit [-->] else { disengage; }
    }

    can inspect with Task entry {
        if self.first_task == "" {
            self.first_task = here.title;
        }

        if here.title == self.first_task and len(self.order) > 0 {
            self.cycles += 1;
            self.order.append(here.title);
            print("recording: cycle found; no exception is thrown");
            disengage;
        } else {
            self.order.append(here.title);
            visit [->:DependsOn:->] else { disengage; }
        }
    }
}

walker StrictTopo {
    has first_task: str = "";
    has order: list[str] = [];
    has aborted: bool = False;

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can enter with Workflow entry {
        visit [-->] else { disengage; }
    }

    can inspect with Task entry {
        if self.first_task == "" {
            self.first_task = here.title;
        }

        if here.title == self.first_task and len(self.order) > 0 {
            self.aborted = True;
            print("strict: CycleError policy aborts on the first cycle");
            disengage;
        } else {
            self.order.append(here.title);
            visit [->:DependsOn:->] else { disengage; }
        }
    }
}

with entry {
    workflow = Workflow(title="orchestrator", year=2024);
    a = Task(title="s0", year=1);
    b = Task(title="s1", year=2);

    root ++> workflow;
    workflow ++> a;
    a +>:DependsOn(reason="s0 waits for s1"):+> b;
    b +>:DependsOn(reason="s1 waits for s0"):+> a;

    recording = root spawn RecordingTopo();
    print("recording order:", recording.order);
    print("recording cycles:", recording.cycles);

    strict = root spawn StrictTopo();
    print("strict order:", strict.order);
    print("strict aborted:", strict.aborted);
}
```

