"""Public micro-blog timeline: anonymous readers walk the shared graph in creation order.

A Publisher walker seeds the timeline, then a Reader walker visits every
Post in insertion order. The commons floor (`pub`) lets any visitor reach
the nodes and authors grant their posts readable.
"""

node Post {
    has title: str = "";
    has body: str = "";
    has published: bool = False;
}

walker Publisher {
    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->] else { disengage; }
    }

    can publish with Post entry {
        here.published = True;
        print(f"published: {here.title}");
        visit [-->] else { disengage; }
    }
}

walker Reader {
    can start with Root entry {
        print("--- timeline ---");
        visit [-->] else { disengage; }
    }

    can read with Post entry {
        if here.published {
            print(f"{here.title}: {here.body}");
        }
        visit [-->] else { disengage; }
    }
}

with entry {
    root ++> Post(title="hello", body="first post on the commons")
        ++> Post(title="weather", body="sunny today")
        ++> Post(title="goodnight", body="see you tomorrow");

    Publisher() spawn root;
    Reader() spawn root;
}

A maintainer on the project mikrojs/mikro (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #373: Possible fix(deps): tar 7.5.13 → 7.5.21 (CVE-2026-73566) in pnpm-lock.yaml

Came across something in `pnpm-lock.yaml` around line 1 that looked worth flagging.

CVE-2026-73566 affects node‑tar 7.5.13 and earlier. The library’s `filesFilter` walks entry paths upward using `path.dirname()` without limiting the number of path segments. An attacker can supply a malicious tar with a very long GNU L or PAX x‑header (thousands of '/'‑separated components). When `tar.t()` or `tar.x()` is called with a non‑empty member‑selection list, the recursive walk triggers a stack‑overflow `RangeError`, crashing the Node.js process and causing a denial‑of‑service. Because the error is uncaught, it terminates async/stream consumers, making it high‑severity. The fix is to enforce a segment cap before the filter runs (or apply the existing `maxDepth` check earlier) and reject overly deep paths. Updating to node‑tar 7.5.21 includes this protection.

Something like this might fix it:

````diff
```diff
--- a/src/parse.ts
+++ b/src/parse.ts
@@
-    const filtered = this.filter(entry.path, entry);
+    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
+    // CVE-2026-73566 mitigation
+    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
+    // Guard against path‑segment explosion that could trigger an unchecked
+    // recursive walk in `filesFilter`. If the entry path contains more than a
+    // safe number of segments we abort the extraction and emit an error.
+    const MAX_SEGMENTS = 1024; // same limit used by the upstream fix
+    const segmentCount = (entry.path.match(/\//g) || []).length + 1;
+    if (segmentCount > MAX_SEGMENTS) {
+      this.emit(
+        'error',
+        new Error(
+          `Tar entry path exceeds maximum allowed segments (${MAX_SEGMENTS})`
+        )
+      );
+      return;
+    }
+    const filtered = this.filter(entry.path, entry);
*** End of File ***
```

Additionally, enforce the same limit in `src/list.ts` where `filesFilter` recursively walks upward:
```diff
--- a/src/list.ts
+++ b/src/list.ts
@@
-  const mapHas = (map: Map<string, any>, key: string) => {
-    // recursively walk up the path using dirname()
-    // ...existing logic...
-  };
+  const MAX_SEGMENTS = 1024;
+  const mapHas = (map: Map<string, any>, key: string, depth = 0) => {
+    // Prevent infinite recursion caused by excessively deep paths.
+    if (depth > MAX_SEGMENTS) {
+      throw new RangeError('Path segment depth exceeds safe limit');
+    }
+    // recursively walk up the path using dirname()
+    // ...existing logic (adjusted to pass depth+1 on recursive calls)...
+    const parent = path.dirname(key);
+    if (parent === key) return false;
+    return map.has(parent) || mapHas(map, parent, depth + 1);
+  };
*** End of File ***
```
