Issue #30: Lifecycle / Disposal Implementation

## Objective

Implement controlled asset lifecycle and disposal functionality as the next product block after completion of the multi-tenant isolation rehearsal.

This issue must build on the current tenant-safe architecture and preserve all historical assignment, transfer, project, payment and audit evidence.

## Phase 1 — domain review

Before implementation:

- inventory all current `current_status` values and status handling;
- identify legacy spelling/case variants;
- identify every route, report, filter, Telegram/public view and Excel sync path that depends on asset status;
- document the lifecycle transition model;
- confirm whether `disposed` already exists anywhere in schema/code even if no current asset uses it.

Do not normalize legacy values silently.

## Disposal model

A disposal must be a controlled lifecycle event, not merely a free-form status edit.

At minimum capture:

- asset;
- tenant_id;
- previous lifecycle status;
- disposal date;
- disposal reason / method;
- responsible actor;
- optional reference/document note;
- resulting status;
- complete audit evidence.

Existing historical assignments, transfers and funding must remain readable.

## Required invariants

A disposed asset must not:

- receive a new active assignment;
- be transferred as an active asset;
- be treated as available stock;
- silently return to an active lifecycle state without an explicit supported reversal workflow.

All reads and writes must remain tenant-scoped.

Cross-tenant references must fail closed before writes.

## UI

Provide a deliberate disposal action from the admin asset workflow.

The operator must receive a clear confirmation before committing the lifecycle change.

The asset detail page must clearly display disposed state and disposal information.

Historical information remains visible.

## Audit

Disposal must create tenant-aware audit evidence containing enough information to reconstruct:

- who performed the action;
- when;
- previous state;
- new state;
- reason/method.

## Public / Telegram behavior

Define and test intended behavior for disposed assets on:

- canonical public `/t/{tenant_id}/...` routes;
- Tenant #1 legacy public routes;
- Telegram lookup;
- Mini App;
- asset-list reports/PDF.

Do not expose another tenant's lifecycle information.

## Excel sync / import

Define how disposal state interacts with Excel synchronization.

A stale workbook/import must not accidentally reactivate a disposed asset without an explicitly approved lifecycle rule.

## Migration safety

No production migration is permitted until:

- schema/data-impact analysis is complete;
- STAGING migration is rehearsed;
- rollback procedure exists;
- Tenant #1 backward compatibility is demonstrated.

## Required automated evidence

Tests must cover at least:

- valid disposal;
- required field validation;
- tenant scoping;
- cross-tenant disposal attempt rejected;
- disposed asset cannot receive a new assignment;
- disposed asset cannot be transferred as active;
- audit evidence;
- asset detail/report visibility;
- public route behavior;
- Telegram/Mini App behavior as applicable;
- Excel sync interaction;
- Tenant #1 legacy behavior;
- full regression suite.

## STAGING acceptance

Before production consideration:

1. deploy to STAGING;
2. dispose a synthetic/regression asset;
3. verify DB lifecycle state;
4. verify audit;
5. verify assignment/transfer restrictions;
6. verify canonical public behavior;
7. verify Tenant #1 remains unchanged;
8. run full regression;
9. obtain explicit GO for production.

## Out of scope unless separately approved

- physical asset deletion;
- deletion of historical assignment/transfer/audit records;
- production execution during implementation;
- broad status normalization unrelated to lifecycle/disposal.

## Definition of done

Lifecycle/disposal is considered complete only when the workflow is tenant-safe, historically traceable, auditable, regression-tested and rehearsed successfully in STAGING.

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

"""Tenant-safe asset disposal with immutable lifecycle and audit evidence."""

node Tenant {
    has tenant_id: str = "";
    has display_name: str = "";
}

node Asset {
    has title: str = "";
    has year: int = 0;
    has available: bool = True;
    has tenant_id: str = "";
    has lifecycle_status: str = "active";
    has disposal_date: str = "";
    has disposal_reason: str = "";
    has disposal_method: str = "";
    has disposed_by: str = "";
    has reference_note: str = "";

    def disposal_allowed(
        requested_tenant: str,
        reason: str,
        disposal_method: str
    ) -> bool {
        if self.tenant_id != requested_tenant {
            return False;
        }
        if self.lifecycle_status != "active" {
            return False;
        }
        if reason == "" {
            return False;
        }
        if disposal_method == "" {
            return False;
        }
        return True;
    }

    def can_receive_assignment() -> bool {
        return self.lifecycle_status == "active" and self.available;
    }

    def can_transfer() -> bool {
        return self.lifecycle_status == "active" and self.available;
    }
}

node DisposalEvent {
    has tenant_id: str = "";
    has previous_status: str = "";
    has disposal_date: str = "";
    has reason: str = "";
    has method: str = "";
    has actor: str = "";
    has reference_note: str = "";
    has resulting_status: str = "disposed";
}

node AuditRecord {
    has tenant_id: str = "";
    has action: str = "asset_disposal";
    has actor: str = "";
    has occurred_at: str = "";
    has previous_status: str = "";
    has new_status: str = "";
    has reason: str = "";
    has method: str = "";
}

edge Contains { }
edge DisposedBy { }
edge AuditedBy { }

walker LifecycleAuditor {
    has tenant_id: str = "";
    has hop_count: int = 0;
    has max_hops: int = 8;
    has audit_ok: bool = False;

    can begin with Root entry {
        visit [-->] else { disengage; }
    }

    can inspect_tenant with Tenant entry {
        self.hop_count = self.hop_count + 1;
        if self.hop_count > self.max_hops {
            print("refused: lifecycle walk exceeded its depth limit");
            disengage;
        }
        if here.tenant_id != self.tenant_id {
            print("refused: cross-tenant lifecycle branch");
            disengage;
        }
        print(f"tenant scope verified: {here.display_name}");
        visit [->:Contains:->] else { disengage; }
    }

    can inspect_asset with Asset entry {
        self.hop_count = self.hop_count + 1;
        if self.hop_count > self.max_hops {
            print("refused: lifecycle walk exceeded its depth limit");
            disengage;
        }
        if here.tenant_id != self.tenant_id {
            print("refused: cross-tenant asset");
            disengage;
        }

        assignment_allowed = here.can_receive_assignment();
        transfer_allowed = here.can_transfer();
        print(f"asset: {here.title} status={here.lifecycle_status}");
        print(f"new assignment allowed: {assignment_allowed}");
        print(f"active transfer allowed: {transfer_allowed}");

        if here.lifecycle_status != "disposed" {
            print("refused: asset has no controlled disposal event");
            disengage;
        }
        visit [->:DisposedBy:->] else { disengage; }
    }

    can inspect_disposal with DisposalEvent entry {
        self.hop_count = self.hop_count + 1;
        if self.hop_count > self.max_hops {
            print("refused: lifecycle walk exceeded its depth limit");
            disengage;
        }
        if here.tenant_id != self.tenant_id {
            print("refused: cross-tenant disposal evidence");
            disengage;
        }
        if here.resulting_status != "disposed" {
            print("refused: invalid disposal result");
            disengage;
        }
        print(f"disposal: {here.reason} via {here.method}, actor={here.actor}");
        visit [->:AuditedBy:->] else { disengage; }
    }

    can record_audit with AuditRecord entry {
        evidence_complete = (
            here.tenant_id == self.tenant_id
            and here.previous_status != ""
            and here.new_status == "disposed"
            and here.actor != ""
            and here.occurred_at != ""
            and here.reason != ""
            and here.method != ""
        );
        self.audit_ok = evidence_complete;
        print(f"audit evidence complete: {self.audit_ok}");
        visit [-->] else { disengage; }
    }
}

