A maintainer on the project SharpMUSH/SharpMUSH (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #882: Indirect locks recurse without a depth bound; PennMUSH denies past max_depth

Found while acting on review for #874. Pre-existing on `main` — #874 neither introduces nor fixes it.

## The problem

`SharpMUSHBooleanExpressionVisitor.VisitIndirectExpr` resolves the target object, reads its lock, and evaluates it:

```csharp
var lockData = targetObj.Object().Locks.GetValueOrDefault(lockType, new SharpLockData("#TRUE"));
return await services.EvaluateLock(lockData.LockString, targetObj, unlockerObj);
```

`ILockEvaluationServices.EvaluateLock` goes to `LockService.Evaluate`, which compiles that lock string and runs it — which can reach `VisitIndirectExpr` again. Nothing counts the depth and nothing tracks which objects have already been visited.

So two objects that name each other recurse until the stack is gone:

```
@lock/basic #1=@#2
@lock/basic #2=@#1
```

Any check of `#1`'s Basic lock — movement, `@pemit`, `CanInteract`, channel visibility — then walks `#1 → #2 → #1 → …`. A self-referential single object (`@lock/basic #1=@#1`) does it on its own. Both are settable by anyone who controls the objects, and a `StackOverflowException` cannot be caught: it takes the process down, not the command.

Grepping the lock path (`LockService`, `LockEvaluationServices`, `BooleanExpressionParser`, the visitor) finds no depth counter, no visited set, and no limit of any kind.

## What PennMUSH does

`eval_boolexp` (`src/boolexp.c:394`) counts and denies:

```c
static int boolexp_recursion = 0;
static bool recurse_err_shown = 0;

if (boolexp_recursion == 0)
  recurse_err_shown = 0;
...
if (boolexp_recursion > MAX_DEPTH) {
  if (!recurse_err_shown) {
    recurse_err_shown = 1;
    notify(player, T("Too much recursion in lock!"));
  }
  return 0;
}
```

with the counter incremented and decremented around each nested evaluation, and `MAX_DEPTH` being `options.max_depth` (`hdrs/conf.h:460`). Exceeding it denies the lock and tells the player once per outermost evaluation.

## Shape of a fix

- `max_depth` already exists here — `LimitOptions.MaxDepth`, read from `max_depth` in `ReadPennMUSHConfig.cs:178`. It is the same option PennMUSH reads, so no new configuration is needed.
- PennMUSH can use a `static int` because it is single-threaded. SharpMUSH's queue is `MaxConcurrency = 1` but evaluation is `async` and interleaves, so the counter wants `AsyncLocal<int>` (or an explicit depth carried through `ILockEvaluationServices.EvaluateLock`) rather than a static.
- Deny on exceeding, matching PennMUSH — and consistent with how the parser already treats a lock it cannot make sense of.
- The "notify once" half needs a route to the player that the compiled delegate does not currently have; denying silently would already remove the crash if that wants to be a second step.

## Tests worth having

- Two mutually-indirect locks terminate and deny, rather than exhausting the stack.
- A self-referential indirect lock does the same.
- A legitimately nested chain shorter than `max_depth` still evaluates correctly, so the bound does not deny honest locks.

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

"""Bound indirect-lock traversal demo with typed edges and explicit depth denial."""

node GameObject {
    has title: str = "";
    has year: int = 0;
    has available: bool = True;
}

node LockObject(GameObject) {
    has lock_expression: str = "";
}

edge StartsAudit { }

edge IndirectLock {
    has lock_type: str = "Basic";
}

walker LockAudit {
    has max_depth: int = 3;
    has depth: int = 0;
    has denied: bool = False;

    can begin with Root entry {
        print("Starting indirect-lock evaluation");
        visit [->:StartsAudit:->] else {
            disengage;
        }
    }

    can evaluate with LockObject entry {
        if self.depth >= self.max_depth {
            self.denied = True;
            print(f"DENY {here.title}: maximum lock depth reached");
            disengage;
        } else {
            self.depth += 1;
            print(f"Evaluating {here.title}: {here.lock_expression}");
            visit [->:IndirectLock:->] else {
                print(f"ALLOW {here.title}: lock chain ended");
                disengage;
            }
        }
    }
}

with entry {
    mutual_a = LockObject(
        title="Object #1",
        lock_expression="@#2"
    );
    mutual_b = LockObject(
        title="Object #2",
        lock_expression="@#1"
    );
    mutual_a +>:IndirectLock(lock_type="Basic"):+> mutual_b;
    mutual_b +>:IndirectLock(lock_type="Basic"):+> mutual_a;
    root +>:StartsAudit:+> mutual_a;
    root spawn LockAudit(max_depth=3);

    self_reference = LockObject(
        title="Self-referential object",
        lock_expression="@#self"
    );
    self_reference +>:IndirectLock(lock_type="Basic"):+> self_reference;
    self_reference spawn LockAudit(max_depth=2);

    chain_start = LockObject(
        title="Nested chain start",
        lock_expression="@#middle"
    );
    chain_middle = LockObject(
        title="Nested chain middle",
        lock_expression="@#end"
    );
    chain_end = LockObject(
        title="Nested chain end",
        lock_expression="#TRUE"
    );
    chain_start +>:IndirectLock(lock_type="Basic"):+> chain_middle;
    chain_middle +>:IndirectLock(lock_type="Basic"):+> chain_end;
    chain_start spawn LockAudit(max_depth=4);
