A maintainer on the project zozo123/threebody-closing-the-open (Python) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #146: [RELEASE][CLAIMS] Enforce one epistemic truth across graph, manifest, README and manuscript

# Single release-facing claim system: enforce one epistemic truth across graph, manifest, README and manuscript

## Overview

Build a single release-facing claim system so stale or over-strong scientific wording becomes impossible, and machine artifacts must agree with each other **before prose is generated**. Every README/manuscript/release claim must derive from current canonical evidence **and its semantic/epistemic level**, not from an older graph snapshot or a manually preserved sentence.

Each claim must be bound to:
- exact wording/scope;
- source evidence artifact digests;
- criterion/search semantics;
- source commit/spec/gates;
- epistemic level;
- unresolved invalidators.

## Use Cases

- Prevent public/static text from claiming an endpoint is `bound`/`certified`/`resolved`/`validated` when canonical assembly evidence does not support it (endpoint resolutions must be applied).
- Reserve branch-level language by semantic class: strict root/localization; sampled 2+ point piece/polyline; continuously traced object; canonically bound incidence; independently reproduced physical branch. A finite sweep-linked polyline does not become a continuously certified physical branch just because every constituent root passes a residual gate.
- Fail the build before LaTeX if README prose, manuscript claims, release manifest, generated claims, canonical graph fields, and claim source artifacts disagree materially.

## Current implementation

The Discovery release workflow currently runs:
1. `build_discovery_release.py --validate-only`;
2. generated status/claims;
3. LaTeX manuscript build;
4. only afterward `--require-solved`.

`--validate-only` checks the release manifest but does not lint static README/paper prose against current graph/source evidence; assurance validation loads only on `--require-solved`. Thus the workflow can successfully build a PDF containing stale/contradictory prose even though final solved publication correctly fails.

## Pain (current contradictions on `main`)

### 1. Canonical endpoint truth vs README / manuscript / release manifest

Canonical assembly is fail-closed and current endpoint-resolution evidence does **not** support the old component-12 organizer claim. Yet current public/static text still says variants of:
- README: `plus_one component-12 high ... bound ... by a certified variational step`;
- manuscript abstract/body: six `certified label-invisible event-sign polylines`, three finite-lattice ends, and component-12 high bound to `mixed_principal_right`;
- `research/DISCOVERY_RELEASE.json` decision summary repeats the same bound/certified component-12 claim.

The manuscript is internally contradictory too: a later paragraph says the same component-12 end is **not** already bound by a classification artifact and shows its attachment is threshold-dependent.

Commit `6bade08` fixes the resolver producer that generated the degenerate low/high evidence, but that only explains why the old claim was invalid; it does not retroactively make the old artifact a valid incidence certificate.

**Rule:** until new side-specific continuous evidence is accepted by canonical assembly, no public/generated/static text may call that endpoint `bound`, `certified`, `resolved`, `validated`, or equivalent.

### 2. Sampled polylines vs continuously certified physical branches

The six historical label-invisible sweep edges in the graph come from `full_domain_event_sign_sweep` with float64 root evidence. They are useful sampled mechanism polylines/candidate topology, but public text currently calls them **certified polylines**.

### 3. Mixed-organizer epistemic level is inconsistent across machine artifacts

Artifact `V1_MIXED_CANONICAL_PRINCIPAL_LEFT_2026-08-15.json` says `claim_status = high_precision_supported`. `research/DISCOVERY_RELEASE.json` states the three headline mixed-organizer artifacts are one rung below `independently_reproduced` and must **not** be worded as independent reproductions. But `V1_CRITICAL_GRAPH.json` marks the headline mixed nodes with `status = independently_reproduced` — a machine-machine epistemic contradiction; the graph must not silently upgrade the source artifact's claim class.

Required invariant: `derived_claim_level <= minimum(authoritative parent evidence levels)`. If two artifacts use different vocabularies, define an explicit mapping table and test it. Do not infer level from implementation names like "independent Julia stack."

### Precise semantic invalidation

When an upstream fact changes, downgrade exactly the dependent claims — do not turn unrelated resolved facts red. Minimum semantic distinctions:
- historical S/U transition enumeration;
- finite event-sign search;
- corrected isolated root;
- sampled multi-point piece;
- continuous object;
- canonically accepted incidence;
- high-precision supported object;
- independently reproduced object;
- publication-supported claim;
- bounded numerical scope;
- theorem-grade/global exclusion, if ever separately established.

### Root/source identity is also a claim semantic

Historical census IDs, supplemental IDs, raw component IDs and canonical namespaced component IDs are different spaces. A release claim naming a supplemental object must bind the exact source artifact/digest and stable physical identity; a bare integer is not enough.

## Requirements

### Pre-manuscript public-truth gate

Add a gate that checks at least:
- README static scientific-status paragraph;
- `paper/main.tex` static scientific claims;
- `research/DISCOVERY_RELEASE.json` decision/gate text;
- generated claims;
- canonical graph fields;
- underlying claim source artifacts;
- assurance/epistemic policy.

The build must fail before LaTeX if these disagree materially.

### Required hostile regressions

1. `endpoint_resolutions_applied == 0` while prose says "bound/certified endpoint" → **pre-build failure**.
2. source artifact `high_` (truncated in issue; continue with the level-mismatch class of regression: graph status exceeding source artifact claim status must fail).

### Claim registry as typed interface (grooming addendum)

The claim registry (#146) should ingest four new upstream dimensions:
- **#154 spec hash** — what exact mathematical problem was solved;
- **#155 source-data lineage** — what exact catalog/data rows were used;
- **#156 literature map** — what prior work supports/challenges each claim and what novelty remains;
- **#152 systematics envelope** — whether the wording survives known numerical/formulation/platform variations.

Extend the claim schema with fields such as:
- `spec_hash`;
- `source_dataset_hash`;
- `sheet_scope` / `sheet_ids`;
- `quantifiers` (`for_all`, `exists`, uniqueness scope, domain);
- `literature_status` / primary source IDs;
- `systematics_status` + weakest margin;
- `calibration_status` (from #151);
- `independence_scorecard` (from #144);
- `proof_obligation_ids` (for #147).

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

"""Epistemic claim registry: one truth across graph, manifest, README and manuscript.

Models the essential scenario from issue #146: a release-facing claim system
that ties each public claim to its evidence artifact, epistemic level, and
invalidator list, then walks the graph to flag any wording that exceeds what
its source evidence actually supports (e.g. "bound"/"certified" prose whose
underlying endpoint_resolutions_applied count is zero).
"""

node Claim {
    has title: str = "";
    has wording: str = "";
    has level: str = "raw";
    has published: bool = False;
}

node Artifact {
    has title: str = "";
    has digest: str = "";
    has claim_status: str = "raw";
    has endpoint_resolutions_applied: int = 0;
}

node Invalidator {
    has title: str = "";
    has reason: str = "";
}

edge SupportedBy {
    has weight: float = 1.0;
}

edge InvalidatedBy {
}

node Manifest {
    has title: str = "";
    has decision_text: str = "";
}

node Prose {
    has title: str = "";
    has body: str = "";
    has kind: str = "readme";
}

walker TruthGate {
    has failures: int = 0;
    has visited: list[str] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can check_manifest with Manifest entry {
        print(f"[manifest] {here.title}: {here.decision_text}");
        if "bound" in here.decision_text or "certified" in here.decision_text {
            print("  [manifest-claim] prose claims bound/certified without checking evidence");
            self.failures += 1;
        }
        visit [-->];
    }

    can check_prose with Prose entry {
        print(f"[prose:{here.kind}] {here.title}: {here.body}");
        if "bound" in here.body or "certified" in here.body {
            print(f"  [prose-claim] '{here.title}' asserts bound/certified");
            self.failures += 1;
        }
        visit [-->];
    }

    can check_claim with Claim entry {
        if here.title not in self.visited {
            self.visited.append(here.title);
        }
        level_seen = "raw";
        inv_count = 0;
        for child in [->:SupportedBy:->] {
            if isinstance(child, Artifact) {
                level_seen = child.claim_status;
                if child.endpoint_resolutions_applied == 0 and ("bound" in here.wording or "certified" in here.wording) {
                    print(f"  [violation] claim '{here.title}' wording is bound/certified but endpoint_resolutions_applied == 0");
                    self.failures += 1;
                }
            }
        }
        for child in [->:InvalidatedBy:->] {
            if isinstance(child, Invalidator) {
                inv_count += 1;
            }
        }
        print(f"[claim] {here.title} level={here.level} artifact_level={level_seen} invalidators={inv_count}");
        visit [-->];
    }
}

with entry {
    artifact = Artifact(
        title="V1_MIXED_CANONICAL_PRINCIPAL_LEFT",
        digest="sha256:deadbeef",
        claim_status="high_precision_supported",
        endpoint_resolutions_applied=0
    );

    main_claim = Claim(
        title="component-12 mixed_principal_left",
        wording="component-12 high bound to mixed_principal_right by a certified variational step",
        level="high",
        published=True
    );

    invalidator = Invalidator(
        title="no side-specific continuous evidence yet",
        reason="canonical assembly has not accepted new continuous evidence"
    );

    readme = Prose(
        title="README status",
        body="plus_one component-12 high ... bound ... by a certified variational step",
        kind="readme"
    );

    paper = Prose(
        title="manuscript abstract",
        body="six certified label-invisible event-sign polylines",
        kind="paper"
    );

    manifest = Manifest(
        title="DISCOVERY_RELEASE.json",
        decision_text="component-12 high bound to mixed_principal_right"
    );

    root ++> manifest;
    root ++> readme;
    root ++> paper;
    root ++> main_claim;
    main_claim +>:SupportedBy:+> artifact;
    main_claim +>:InvalidatedBy:+> invalidator;

    print("=== TruthGate walk: single epistemic truth audit ===");
    gate = TruthGate();
    root spawn gate;
    print(f"=== TruthGate failures: {gate.failures} (expect > 0: prose claims bound/certified while endpoint_resolutions_applied == 0) ===");
}

"""Depot network: spawn one query from root, walk every depot and its stores,
and flag low-stock stores (under 20 units)."""

node Store {
    has name: str = "";
    has stock: int = 0;
}

node Depot {
    has name: str = "";

    def low_stores() -> list {
        result = [];
        for s in [-->] {
            if isinstance(s, Store) and s.stock < 20 {
                result.append(s);
            }
        }
        return result;
    }
}

edge Supplies {}

walker StockAuditor {
    has threshold: int = 20;
    has flagged: list = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can audit_depot with Depot entry {
        report_here = here;
        for low in report_here.low_stores() {
            self.flagged.append(low);
            print(f"[LOW] {low.name} stock={low.stock} via depot {report_here.name}");
        }
        visit [-->];
    }

    can note_store with Store entry {
        visit [-->];
    }
}

with entry {
    d_a = Depot(name="North-Depot");
    d_b = Depot(name="South-Depot");

    s1 = Store(name="Store-1", stock=45);
    s2 = Store(name="Store-2", stock=12);
    s3 = Store(name="Store-3", stock=8);
    s4 = Store(name="Store-4", stock=33);
    s5 = Store(name="Store-5", stock=17);

    d_a +>:Supplies:+> s1;
    d_a +>:Supplies:+> s2;
    d_a +>:Supplies:+> s3;
    d_b +>:Supplies:+> s4;
    d_b +>:Supplies:+> s5;

    root ++> d_a ++> d_b;

    auditor = StockAuditor(threshold=20);
    root spawn auditor;

    print(f"Total flagged: {len(auditor.flagged)} store(s) below 20 units");
}

"""Paged KV block allocation model.

Models the sparse-attention compressed-KV scenario: a query row references
two physical KV blocks via ``cmp_sparse_indices``. The bug is that PA_ND
issued a single strided DMA starting from the lower physical address,
so the merged K/V row order depended on which physical block was
allocated first. Logical order from ``cmp_sparse_indices`` must win.

We model this with one walker ability: ``Auditor`` walks the page
table and, for each compressed-KV pair slot, prints the merged row
order under both the *buggy* physical-order rule and the *fixed*
logical-order rule.
"""

# A physical paged KV block. Each block holds ``block_size`` rows of K/V
# data; the row values themselves are intact in either order.
node KvBlock {
    has block_id: int = 0;
    has block_size: int = 0;
    has gm_offset: int = 0;
    has rows: list[int] = [];
}

# A compressed sparse index referencing two physical blocks in the order
# the operator *logically* consumes them.
node SparseIndex {
    has index_id: int = 0;
    has slot: int = 0;
}

# The query row that owns a list of ``SparseIndex`` entries.
node QueryRow {
    has row_id: int = 0;
    has total_pairs: int = 0;
}

edge PhysicalAlloc {
    has allocated_at: int = 0;
}

edge LogicalPair {
    has pair_slot: int = 0;
}

walker Auditor {
    has total_slots: int = 0;
    has current_slot: int = -1;

    can start with Root entry {
        self.total_slots = 0;
        self.current_slot = -1;
        visit [-->];
    }

    can gather with QueryRow entry {
        print(f"QueryRow {here.row_id} references {here.total_pairs} compressed-KV pairs");
        visit [->:LogicalPair:->];
    }

    can pair with SparseIndex entry {
        self.current_slot = here.slot;
        self.total_slots = self.total_slots + 1;
        blocks = [here->:PhysicalAlloc:->];
        if len(blocks) >= 2 {
            b1 = blocks[0];
            b2 = blocks[1];
            # Logical order (FIX): concatenate in cmp_sparse_indices order.
            merged_logical = list(b1.rows) + list(b2.rows);
            # Physical order (BUG): pick the block with the lower gm_offset first.
            if b1.gm_offset <= b2.gm_offset {
                first_block = b1;
                second_block = b2;
            } else {
                first_block = b2;
                second_block = b1;
            }
            merged_physical = list(first_block.rows) + list(second_block.rows);
            print(
                f"  [slot={self.current_slot}] BUG physical-order merged = {merged_physical}"
            );
            print(
                f"  [slot={self.current_slot}] FIX logical-order merged  = {merged_logical}"
            );
        } else {
            print(f"  [slot={self.current_slot}] fewer than 2 physical blocks attached");
        }
        visit [-->];
    }
}

with entry {
    # Three physical paged KV blocks; gm_offset simulates runtime allocator state.
    blk_a = KvBlock(block_id=0, block_size=3, gm_offset=100, rows=[1, 2, 3]);
    blk_b = KvBlock(block_id=1, block_size=3, gm_offset=500, rows=[4, 5, 6]);
    blk_c = KvBlock(block_id=2, block_size=3, gm_offset=900, rows=[7, 8, 9]);
    blk_d = KvBlock(block_id=3, block_size=3, gm_offset=1300, rows=[10, 11, 12]);

    # Two compressed-sparse pairs in the *logical* order given by cmp_sparse_indices.
    idx_0 = SparseIndex(index_id=0, slot=0);
    idx_1 = SparseIndex(index_id=1, slot=1);

    # Query row that owns both indices. total_pairs = 2 per the issue scenario.
    qr = QueryRow(row_id=629, total_pairs=2);

    # Wire the graph: root -> QueryRow -> SparseIndex -LogicalPair-> SparseIndex.
    root ++> qr ++> idx_0 ++> idx_1;

    # Logical pair edges connect indices to their next sibling.
    idx_0 +>:LogicalPair(pair_slot=0):+> idx_1;

    # Physical block allocation. Notice blk_a has a *lower* gm_offset than blk_b,
    # and blk_c has a *lower* gm_offset than blk_d -- exactly the kind of
    # allocation state that permutes the buggy merged row order.
    idx_0 +>:PhysicalAlloc(allocated_at=100):+> blk_a;
    idx_0 +>:PhysicalAlloc(allocated_at=500):+> blk_b;

    idx_1 +>:PhysicalAlloc(allocated_at=900):+> blk_c;
    idx_1 +>:PhysicalAlloc(allocated_at=1300):+> blk_d;

    # A second allocation layout for the same logical indices, demonstrating
    # that flipping the physical addresses is what changes the buggy output.
    alt_blk_x = KvBlock(block_id=10, block_size=3, gm_offset=2500, rows=[1, 2, 3]);
    alt_blk_y = KvBlock(block_id=11, block_size=3, gm_offset=2200, rows=[4, 5, 6]);
    idx_0 +>:PhysicalAlloc(allocated_at=2200):+> alt_blk_y;
    idx_0 +>:PhysicalAlloc(allocated_at=2500):+> alt_blk_x;

    print("=== First allocator layout (blk_a < blk_b by gm_offset) ===");
    root spawn Auditor();

    # Now flip the physical block layout for idx_0 to show the BUG
    # changes while the FIX stays the same.
    idx_0 del --> blk_a;
    idx_0 del --> blk_b;
    idx_0 +>:PhysicalAlloc(allocated_at=800):+> blk_b;
    idx_0 +>:PhysicalAlloc(allocated_at=300):+> blk_a;

    print("=== Second allocator layout (blk_b < blk_a by gm_offset) ===");
    root spawn Auditor();
}

"""Sandbox seed router: model the AniList locale-routing dependency and
walk the cache graph with a walker that traces the cold-start path the
spec implicitly relied on, demonstrating why hand-rolled adjacency forces
specs to depend on live fetches instead of typed-edge traversal.
"""

# --- Node archetypes ----------------------------------------------------------

node Anime {
    has anilist_id: int = 0;
    has title: str = "";
    has source: str = "";     # "seeded" or "anilist-live"
    has available: bool = True;
}

node LocaleRoute {
    has code: str = "";       # "en", "zh-Hant", ...
    has path_template: str = "";
}

node Spec {
    has name: str = "";
    has network_dependent: bool = False;
}

node Runner {
    has label: str = "";
    has warmed: bool = False;
}

# --- Typed edges (relationships that carry meaning) ---------------------------

edge SeededFixture {}        # fixture --seeded--> anime (deterministic local data)
edge ColdFetch {}           # anime --cold_fetch--> anime (the slow AniList pull)
edge RoutesTo {}            # locale --routes_to--> anime
edge AssertedBy {}          # spec --asserted_by--> locale (or spec --asserted_by--> anime)
edge WarmUp {}              # runner --warm_up--> route (healthy baseline)

# --- Walkers ------------------------------------------------------------------

walker ColdStartTracer {
    has visited_ids: list = [];
    has cold_start_seen: bool = False;
    has spec_network_dependent: bool = False;

    can begin with Root entry {
        print(f"[trace] starting cold-start walk from root");
        visit [-->];
    }

    can inspect_seed with Anime entry {
        # If this anime was fetched live (not seeded), flag it: a spec that
        # points here will fail when the network is slow or refused.
        self.visited_ids.append(here.anilist_id);
        if here.source == "anilist-live" {
            self.cold_start_seen = True;
            print(f"[trace] /anime/{here.anilist_id} ({here.title}) "
                  f"served via COLD live fetch - spec at risk");
        } else {
            print(f"[trace] /anime/{here.anilist_id} ({here.title}) "
                  f"served via SEEDED fixture - deterministic");
        }
        visit [->:ColdFetch:->];
    }

    can inspect_locale with LocaleRoute entry {
        # Show which anime each locale routes to and whether that target
        # is a seeded fixture or a cold live fetch.
        targets = [here->:RoutesTo:->];
        for target in targets {
            label = "seeded" if target.source == "seeded" else "live-fetch";
            print(f"[trace] locale {here.code} -> /anime/"
                  f"{target.anilist_id} ({label})");
            self.visited_ids.append(target.anilist_id);
            if target.source == "anilist-live" {
                self.cold_start_seen = True;
                self.spec_network_dependent = True;
            }
        }
        visit [-->];
    }

    can inspect_spec with Spec entry {
        # Surface the spec's dependency shape: if a spec asserts a route
        # whose anime is live-fetched, the spec is network-dependent.
        deps = [here->:AssertedBy:->];
        live = False;
        for d in deps {
            sub = [d->:RoutesTo:->];
            for t in sub {
                if t.source == "anilist-live" { live = True; }
            }
        }
        is_live = live;
        here.network_dependent = is_live;
        if live { self.spec_network_dependent = True; }
        risk = "NETWORK-DEPENDENT" if live else "deterministic";
        print(f"[trace] spec '{here.name}' is {risk}");
        visit [-->];
    }

    can inspect_runner with Runner entry {
        print(f"[trace] runner {here.label} warmed={here.warmed}");
        visit [-->];
    }
}

walker CacheInvalidator {
    has purged_ids: list = [];

    can begin with Root entry {
        print(f"[invalidate] cascading cache purge from root");
        visit [-->];
    }

    can purge with Anime entry {
        # Simulate the cascade: drop an anime and any locale routes that
        # pointed at it, forcing a refetch on next access.
        self.purged_ids.append(here.anilist_id);
        print(f"[invalidate] dropped /anime/{here.anilist_id} "
                f"({here.title}); refetch required");
        visit [-->];
    }

    can purge_route with LocaleRoute entry {
        sentinel = -1;
        self.purged_ids.append(sentinel);
        print(f"[invalidate] cleared locale route {here.code} "
                f"({here.path_template})");
        visit [-->];
    }
}

# --- Demonstration graph ------------------------------------------------------
with entry {
    # Seeded fixtures (the good path the issue wants extended).
    a21    = Anime(anilist_id=21,   title="One Piece",            source="seeded");
    a11061 = Anime(anilist_id=11061, title="Cowboy Bebop",        source="seeded");

    # Anime 1535: still left as a live fetch - this is the bug surface.
    a1535  = Anime(anilist_id=1535, title="Naruto",                source="anilist-live");

    en   = LocaleRoute(code="en",     path_template="/en/anime/{id}");
    hant = LocaleRoute(code="zh-Hant", path_template="/zh-Hant/anime/{id}");
    bare = LocaleRoute(code="*",       path_template="/anime/{id}");

    routing_spec = Spec(name="locale-routing.spec.ts");
    episode_spec = Spec(name="episode-display.spec.ts");
    auth_spec    = Spec(name="auth.spec.ts");

    runner = Runner(label="github-runner", warmed=True);

    # Wire the graph.
    root +>:SeededFixture:+> a21;
    root +>:SeededFixture:+> a11061;
    root +>:ColdFetch:+> a1535;

    bare +>:RoutesTo:+> a21;        # /anime/21  -> seeded (fixed by the patch)
    en   +>:RoutesTo:+> a21;        # /en/anime/21
    hant +>:RoutesTo:+> a21;        # /zh-Hant/anime/21

    bare +>:RoutesTo:+> a1535;      # /anime/1535 -> live (still fragile)
    en   +>:RoutesTo:+> a11061;     # /en/anime/11061

    routing_spec +>:AssertedBy:+> bare;
    routing_spec +>:AssertedBy:+> en;
    routing_spec +>:AssertedBy:+> hant;
    episode_spec +>:AssertedBy:+> a21;
    auth_spec    +>:AssertedBy:+> bare;

    runner +>:WarmUp:+> bare;
    runner +>:WarmUp:+> en;
    runner +>:WarmUp:+> hant;

    # Spawn the walker that traces the cold-start path and tells us
    # which specs are still network-dependent.
    tracer = ColdStartTracer();
    root spawn tracer;

    print("");
    print(f"[summary] visited anime ids: {tracer.visited_ids}");
    print(f"[summary] cold-start path observed: {tracer.cold_start_seen}");
    net_dep = tracer.spec_network_dependent;
    summary_label = "NETWORK-DEPENDENT" if net_dep else "deterministic";
    print(f"[summary] locale-routing.spec is {summary_label}");

    # Cascade purge to show the hand-rolled traversal the issue describes.
    print("");
    inv = CacheInvalidator();
    root spawn inv;
    print(f"[summary] purged ids (incl. synthetic route markers): {inv.purged_ids}");
}

"""Delivery routes: model depots and routes, dispatch trucks only for valid loads (2-8 tonnes)."""

node Depot {
    has name: str = "";
}

node Route {
    has label: str = "";
    has weight: float = 0.0;
    has dispatched: bool = False;
}

walker Dispatcher {
    can start with Root entry {
        print("Scanning depots for dispatchable routes...");
        visit [-->];
    }

    can scan with Depot entry {
        print(f"Depot: {here.name}");
        visit [-->];
    }

    can check with Route entry {
        qualifies = here.weight >= 2.0 and here.weight <= 8.0;
        if qualifies {
            here.dispatched = True;
            print(f"  [DISPATCH] {here.label} ({here.weight}t) qualifies");
        } else {
            print(f"  [SKIP] {here.label} ({here.weight}t) outside 2-8t window");
        }
        visit [-->];
    }
}

with entry {
    north = Depot(name="North Hub");
    south = Depot(name="South Hub");

    root ++> north ++>
        Route(label="N-A1", weight=3.5)
        ++> Route(label="N-A2", weight=9.2)
        ++> Route(label="N-A3", weight=1.5);

    root ++> south ++>
        Route(label="S-B1", weight=7.8)
        ++> Route(label="S-B2", weight=2.0)
        ++> Route(label="S-B3", weight=8.0)
        ++> Route(label="S-B4", weight=4.4);

    root spawn Dispatcher();
}

"""Restaurant recommender: finder walker filters by minimum rating."""

node Restaurant {
    has name: str = "";
    has cuisine: str = "";
    has rating: float = 0.0;
}

walker Finder {
    has min_rating: float = 4.5;
    has matches: list[str] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can scan with Restaurant entry {
        if here.rating >= self.min_rating {
            self.matches.append(f"{here.name} ({here.cuisine}, {here.rating})");
        }
        visit [-->];
    }
}

with entry {
    root ++> Restaurant(name="Le Bernardin", cuisine="French", rating=4.8)
        ++> Restaurant(name="Joe's Diner", cuisine="American", rating=3.6)
        ++> Restaurant(name="Sushi Nakazawa", cuisine="Japanese", rating=4.7)
        ++> Restaurant(name="Taqueria La Paz", cuisine="Mexican", rating=4.0)
        ++> Restaurant(name="Cafe Marcel", cuisine="Brunch", rating=3.4);

    date_night = Finder(min_rating=4.5);
    root spawn date_night;
    print(f"Date night picks (4.5+): {date_night.matches}");

    casual_lunch = Finder(min_rating=3.5);
    root spawn casual_lunch;
    print(f"Casual lunch picks (3.5+): {casual_lunch.matches}");
}

"""Pilot dependency hardening for v1.6.1 release epic.

Models the dependency DAG from the v1.6.1 Adoption Hardening epic:
Measurement Protocol feeds three Pilots (Standard / Complex / Large);
each Pilot's results flow into Calibration and Hardening streams;
those streams converge on a Runtime Go/No-Go decision; the decision
gates the Release; the Release opens the Post-release review.

A walker walks the dependency graph, classifying each node by its
admission category (pilot evidence, deterministic bug, setup blocker,
perf regression, compat defect, privacy/security defect) and asserting
that the hard gate (#453 Release Gate) is reachable only when every
upstream node is GO.
"""

node EpicNode {
    has title: str = "";
    has ticket: str = "";
    has status: str = "PENDING";   # PENDING | GO | BLOCKED
    has category: str = "PILOT";   # PILOT | CALIBRATION | HARDENING | STRATEGY | RELEASE
}

edge DependsOn {
    has rationale: str = "";
}

walker EpicAuditor {
    has visited: list[str] = [];
    has blocked: list[str] = [];
    has go_count: int = 0;
    has blocked_count: int = 0;
    has release_unlocked: bool = False;

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [->:DependsOn:->];
    }

    can audit with EpicNode entry {
        key = here.ticket;
        # Cycle safety: re-entry is a defect, mark BLOCKED and stop.
        if key in self.visited {
            here.status = "BLOCKED";
            self.blocked.append(key);
            self.blocked_count += 1;
            print(f"[CYCLE] {key} re-entered -> BLOCKED");
            disengage;
        }
        self.visited.append(key);

        # Domain rule: every upstream must already be GO before this node is GO.
        upstreams = [->:DependsOn:->];
        ready = True;
        for up in upstreams {
            if up.status != "GO" {
                ready = False;
            }
        }

        if ready {
            here.status = "GO";
            self.go_count += 1;
            print(f"[GO]   {here.ticket:>4} {here.title}  ({here.category})");
        } else {
            here.status = "BLOCKED";
            self.blocked.append(here.ticket);
            self.blocked_count += 1;
            print(f"[WAIT] {here.ticket:>4} {here.title}  ({here.category})  waiting on upstreams");
        }

        # Release gate special handling.
        if here.ticket == "#453" and here.status == "GO" {
            self.release_unlocked = True;
        }

        # Forward traverse dependents; dead-end handling prevents hang.
        visit [->:DependsOn:->] else {
            disengage;
        }
    }
}

with entry {
    # Measurement / Pilot layer.
    protocol = EpicNode(title="Pilot Measurement Protocol", ticket="#445", category="PILOT");
    standard = EpicNode(title="Standard NestJS Pilot", ticket="#446", category="PILOT");
    complex  = EpicNode(title="Complex / Monorepo Pilot", ticket="#447", category="PILOT");
    large    = EpicNode(title="Large / OSS Pilot", ticket="#448", category="PILOT");

    # Calibration / Hardening layer (fed by all three pilots).
    calibration = EpicNode(title="Finding Quality Calibration", ticket="#449", category="CALIBRATION");
    dx_hardening = EpicNode(title="Onboarding / DX Hardening", ticket="#450", category="HARDENING");
    perf_hardening = EpicNode(title="Perf / Compat Hardening", ticket="#451", category="HARDENING");

    # Strategy / Release layer.
    go_no_go  = EpicNode(title="Runtime Evidence Go/No-Go", ticket="#452", category="STRATEGY");
    release   = EpicNode(title="v1.6.1 Release Gate", ticket="#453", category="RELEASE");
    post_rel  = EpicNode(title="Post-release / Next-phase Review", ticket="#454", category="STRATEGY");

    # Wire the dependency graph with typed DependsOn edges carrying
    # rationale payloads (mirrors the mermaid flowchart in the issue).
    protocol +>:DependsOn(rationale="protocol feeds every pilot"):+> standard;
    protocol +>:DependsOn(rationale="protocol feeds every pilot"):+> complex;
    protocol +>:DependsOn(rationale="protocol feeds every pilot"):+> large;

    standard +>:DependsOn(rationale="pilot -> calibration"):+> calibration;
    complex  +>:DependsOn(rationale="pilot -> calibration"):+> calibration;
    large    +>:DependsOn(rationale="pilot -> calibration"):+> calibration;

    standard +>:DependsOn(rationale="pilot -> dx hardening"):+> dx_hardening;
    complex  +>:DependsOn(rationale="pilot -> dx hardening"):+> dx_hardening;
    large    +>:DependsOn(rationale="pilot -> dx hardening"):+> dx_hardening;

    standard +>:DependsOn(rationale="pilot -> perf hardening"):+> perf_hardening;
    complex  +>:DependsOn(rationale="pilot -> perf hardening"):+> perf_hardening;
    large    +>:DependsOn(rationale="pilot -> perf hardening"):+> perf_hardening;

    calibration   +>:DependsOn(rationale="calibration -> go/no-go"):+> go_no_go;
    dx_hardening  +>:DependsOn(rationale="dx hardening -> go/no-go"):+> go_no_go;
    perf_hardening +>:DependsOn(rationale="perf hardening -> go/no-go"):+> go_no_go;

    go_no_go +>:DependsOn(rationale="decision gates release"):+> release;
    release  +>:DependsOn(rationale="release opens post-release"):+> post_rel;

    # Attach the dependency tree under root.
    root ++> protocol;

    # Run the auditor. It will walk the DAG, propagating GO forward
    # once each upstream is satisfied, and report the release verdict.
    auditor = EpicAuditor();
    root spawn auditor;

    verdict = "GO" if auditor.release_unlocked else "BLOCKED";
    print("");
    print(f"audited {auditor.go_count} GO, {auditor.blocked_count} BLOCKED");
    print(f"release gate #453 verdict: {verdict}");
}

