"""Package graph reachability walk — models the stack-overflow scenario.

The issue is that a module-collection walk traverses a package's
reachable JS files and recurses into each sub-import. When the
package graph contains a cycle (e.g. a path that resolves back to
its own root), the recursive walk blows the stack because nothing
remembers which packages have already been visited.

This demo models that shape directly:
  - ``Package`` nodes hold a name and the list of reachable sub-packages
    they pull in.
  - ``PackageRef`` nodes are the leaf entries inside a package's
    reachability list (the things the walker recurses into).
  - Typed ``Reaches`` edges carry the relationship from one package
    to the packages it depends on.
  - The ``ReachableCompiler`` walker is the analog of the AOT
    reachability collector. Without an explicit visited set it
    will loop forever on a cyclic graph; with the visited set it
    terminates and reports each package exactly once.

The cycle policy is explicit domain logic: a guard set on the
walker prevents re-entry. The walker also carries a depth cap as
a safety belt so a real bug that produces a deeply-nested (but
not cyclic) graph still terminates.
"""

node Package {
    has name: str = "";
    # The list of sub-package names this package reaches into.
    # Models the "statically_reachable" list the Rust test walks.
    has reachable: list[str] = [];
}

# Leaf entries inside a package's reachability list. In the
# real compiler these are resolved to other packages and then
# walked recursively; here we use them as the trigger that
# causes the walker to advance along a ``Reaches`` edge.
node PackageRef {
    has ref_name: str = "";
}

# A package reaches another package. The relationship carries
# no payload, but giving it a name models the typed traversal
# the idiomatic rewrite should express.
edge Reaches { }

walker ReachableCompiler {
    # Cycle policy: a guard set. A package is processed at most
    # once; subsequent arrivals are skipped.
    has seen: set[str] = set();
    # Safety belt: a depth cap. If the walk exceeds it the
    # walker reports a depth-limit error and disengages. This
    # preserves the "deep but bounded" guard the issue calls
    # for as one of the suggested first diagnostic steps.
    has depth_limit: int = 32;
    # Bookkeeping: ordered list of packages visited, used to
    # produce a single exit report.
    has order: list[str] = [];
    # Bookkeeping: how many times the walker would have
    # re-entered a package without the guard. A non-zero count
    # is the fingerprint of a cycle in the input graph.
    has cycles_skipped: int = 0;
    # Bookkeeping: whether the depth cap was hit.
    has depth_limited: bool = False;

    can begin with Root entry {
        # Kick the walker off the first package the root owns.
        visit [-->];
    }

    can descend with PackageRef entry {
        # Resolve the ref to the package it points at and walk
        # to it. Because we model the relationship as a typed
        # edge we traverse it explicitly with ``visit
        # [here->:Reaches:->]`` rather than chasing a name
        # through an id-keyed map.
        visit [here->:Reaches:->];
    }

    can compile with Package entry {
        # Depth guard: if we are deeper than the cap, stop.
        if len(self.order) >= self.depth_limit {
            self.depth_limited = True;
            disengage;
        }
        # Cycle policy: have we already processed this package?
        if here.name in self.seen {
            self.cycles_skipped += 1;
            # No further ``visit`` from this node — that is
            # what breaks the cycle in the original recursive
            # walk.
        } else {
            self.seen.add(here.name);
            self.order.append(here.name);
            # Recurse into each reachable sub-package by
            # walking through PackageRef leaves and then
            # along the typed ``Reaches`` edge to the next
            # package node.
            visit [-->];
        }
    }
}

with entry {
    # Build a small package graph that includes a cycle:
    #   a -> b -> c -> a
    # which is exactly the shape the issue suggests as the
    # likely culprit ("a package graph that re-enters itself
    # when a subpath resolves back to its own root").
    a = Package(name="a", reachable=["b"]);
    b = Package(name="b", reachable=["c"]);
    c = Package(name="c", reachable=["a"]);

    # For each ``reachable`` entry we materialize a
    # ``PackageRef`` leaf and then connect the source package
    # to the target package with a typed ``Reaches`` edge.
    a ++> PackageRef(ref_name="b");
    a +>:Reaches:+> b;

    b ++> PackageRef(ref_name="c");
    b +>:Reaches:+> c;

    c ++> PackageRef(ref_name="a");
    c +>:Reaches:+> a;

    root ++> a;

    compiler = ReachableCompiler();
    root spawn compiler;

    # Single exit report summarizing what the walk observed.
    # A correct run on a cyclic graph has ``cycles_skipped > 0``
    # and an ``order`` shorter than the theoretical infinite
    # chain — the fingerprint the issue predicts.
    print(f"visited in order: {compiler.order}");
    print(f"cycles skipped  : {compiler.cycles_skipped}");
    print(f"depth limited   : {compiler.depth_limited}");
    print(f"unique packages : {len(compiler.seen)}");
}
